---
import ExplainerExample from './ExplainerExample.astro';
import CascadeExample from './CascadeExample.astro';
import type { CascadeRow } from '../lib/cascade/share';

const row = (selector: string, value: string, extra: Partial<CascadeRow> = {}): CascadeRow => ({
  selector,
  value,
  important: false,
  inline: false,
  layer: null,
  scope: null,
  order: 1,
  ...extra,
});
---

<section class="section section-deferred" aria-labelledby="explainer-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      <h2 id="explainer-title">Specificity and the cascade, explained</h2>
      <p>
        When two rules set the same property on the same element, the browser has to pick one. Most people
        learn that "the more specific selector wins". That's true, but it's only one step of a longer process
        called the cascade. This guide covers both, with examples you can load straight into the tool above.
      </p>

      <nav aria-labelledby="toc-title">
        <h3 id="toc-title">On this page</h3>
        <ul class="toc mt-s">
          <li>
            <a href="#how-calculated">How is CSS specificity calculated?</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#functional">How do :is(), :not(), :has() and :where() count?</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#nth-child-of">What about :nth-child(… of S)?</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#shadow-dom">Shadow DOM selectors</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#nesting">How does nesting affect specificity?</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#cascade-order">The cascade order</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#layers">Does @layer beat specificity?</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#scope">What is @scope proximity?</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#inline">Where do inline styles fit?</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#why-not-applying">Why is my CSS not applying?</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <h2 id="how-calculated">How is CSS specificity calculated?</h2>
      <p>
        Specificity is three separate counts, written <code>(A, B, C)</code>. Look at every simple selector in
        the selector and add it to one column:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A, IDs:</strong> each <code>#id</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>B, classes:</strong> each <code>.class</code>, attribute selector like{' '}
          <code>[type="text"]</code>
          and pseudo-class like <code>:hover</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>C, types:</strong> each element name like <code>p</code> and pseudo-element like
          <code>::before</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The universal selector <code>*</code> and combinators (space, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>+</code>,
        <code>~</code>) add nothing. Namespace prefixes such as <code>svg|</code> add nothing either.
      </p>
      <p>
        To compare two selectors, read the columns from left to right. The first column that differs decides
        the result and the rest never get a look. Columns never carry over: eleven classes don't add up to an
        ID.
      </p>
      <ExplainerExample
        selectors={['#nav a', '.menu .link:hover']}
        caption="One ID beats any number of classes"
      />
      <ExplainerExample
        selectors={['.a.a.a.a.a.a.a.a.a.a.a', '#b']}
        caption="Eleven classes still lose to one ID"
      />
      <p>
        If every column is equal, specificity can't separate them, and the rule written later wins. That's the
        last step of the cascade, <a href="#cascade-order">order of appearance</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Old single-colon pseudo-elements such as <code>a:before</code> still count as pseudo-elements, in the
        third column. A selector list like <code>.a, #b</code> isn't scored as a whole: each selector has its
        own specificity, and the rule uses the one that matched.
      </p>

      <h2 id="functional">How do :is(), :not(), :has() and :where() count?</h2>
      <p>
        <code>:is()</code>, <code>:not()</code> and <code>:has()</code> take the specificity of their
        <strong>most specific argument</strong>. That happens even when the element only matches a weaker
        argument, or, for <code>:not()</code>, matches none of them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>:where()</code> always counts as zero, whatever is inside it. That makes it the best tool for
        writing defaults that are easy to override.
      </p>
      <ExplainerExample
        selectors={['.foo :is(.bar, #baz)', '.qux:where(em, #foo#bar#baz)', ':not(em, strong#foo)']}
        caption="The most specific argument counts; :where() counts nothing"
      />
      <p>
        <code>:is()</code> and <code>:where()</code> also take a forgiving list: an argument the browser can't
        parse is ignored instead of breaking the whole rule. <code>:not()</code> and <code>:has()</code> don't
        forgive.
      </p>

      <h2 id="nth-child-of">What about :nth-child(… of S)?</h2>
      <p>
        <code>:nth-child()</code> and <code>:nth-last-child()</code> count as one pseudo-class. Add the
        <code>of S</code> syntax and they also add the most specific selector in <code>S</code>. Other
        pseudo-classes with arguments, such as <code>:nth-of-type(2)</code> or <code>:lang(en)</code>, count
        as one pseudo-class.
      </p>
      <ExplainerExample
        selectors={[':nth-child(even of li, .item)', ':nth-last-child(odd of #a .b)', ':nth-of-type(2)']}
        caption="One pseudo-class, plus the strongest argument after of"
      />

      <h2 id="shadow-dom">Shadow DOM selectors</h2>
      <p>
        Inside a component's shadow tree, <code>:host</code> counts as a pseudo-class. <code>:host()</code>{' '}
        and
        <code>:host-context()</code> count as a pseudo-class plus their argument. <code>::slotted()</code>{' '}
        counts as a pseudo-element plus its argument, and <code>::part()</code> counts as one pseudo-element.
      </p>
      <ExplainerExample
        selectors={[':host', ':host(#x) .y', '::slotted(span.x)', 'x-foo::part(label):hover']}
        caption="Shadow DOM selectors add their arguments"
      />
      <p>
        Specificity isn't the whole story here. Styles from the page and styles from inside a shadow tree are
        compared at the <a href="#cascade-order">context step</a> of the cascade first: for normal
        declarations the page wins, and for <code>!important</code> the shadow tree wins.
      </p>

      <h2 id="nesting">How does nesting affect specificity?</h2>
      <p>
        In native CSS nesting, <code>&amp;</code> stands for the parent selector wrapped in <code>:is()</code>
        . When the parent is a list, the nested selector takes the specificity of the <em>strongest</em>{' '}
        parent, even for elements that only match a weaker one.
      </p>
      <pre class="code"><code>{`.a, #b {
  & .c { color: red; }
}

/* becomes */
:is(.a, #b) .c { color: red; }  /* (1,1,0), even for .a .c */`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        A nested rule without <code>&amp;</code> behaves as if it started with <code>&amp; </code>. Use the
        <a href="#panel-nesting">nesting expander</a> to paste your own CSS and see the result. Nested
        <code>@media</code> and <code>@supports</code> blocks become separate rules, which can change the
        order of appearance compared with how the code reads.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cascade-order">The cascade order</h2>
      <p>
        Specificity is step five of seven. The browser walks these steps in order and stops at the first one
        that separates the declarations:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Origin and importance.</strong> Transitions, then <code>!important</code> browser, user and
          author styles, then animations, then normal author, user and browser styles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Context.</strong> The page versus a shadow tree.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Element-attached styles.</strong> The <code>style</code> attribute beats rules from
          stylesheets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Layers.</strong> The order of <code>@layer</code> blocks, and unlayered styles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Specificity.</strong> The <code>(A, B, C)</code> comparison.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scope proximity.</strong> How close the <code>@scope</code> root is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Order of appearance.</strong> The last one written wins.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The <a href="#panel-cascade">cascade resolver</a> above walks these steps for your own declarations
        and marks the step where the winner was decided.
      </p>

      <h2 id="layers">Does @layer beat specificity?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. Layers are compared at step four, before specificity gets a vote. For normal declarations, later
        layers beat earlier ones, and styles outside any layer beat all layered styles.
      </p>
      <CascadeExample
        caption="Unlayered beats layered, whatever the specificity"
        layerOrder={['components']}
        code={`@layer components {
  #hero .title { color: navy; }
}
h1 { color: crimson; }`}
        rows={[
          row('#hero .title', 'navy', { layer: 'components', order: 1 }),
          row('h1', 'crimson', { order: 2 }),
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        For <code>!important</code> the order <strong>reverses</strong>: the earliest layer wins, and layered
        <code>!important</code> beats unlayered <code>!important</code>. This lets a low-level reset protect a
        few declarations without fighting everything above it.
      </p>
      <CascadeExample
        caption="!important reverses the layer order"
        layerOrder={['reset', 'components']}
        code={`@layer reset, components;
@layer reset { p { color: red !important; } }
@layer components { .note p { color: blue !important; } }
#intro { color: green !important; }`}
        rows={[
          row('p', 'red', { layer: 'reset', important: true, order: 1 }),
          row('.note p', 'blue', { layer: 'components', important: true, order: 2 }),
          row('#intro', 'green', { important: true, order: 3 }),
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        Calling <code>!important</code> a "cascade layer" is a common mistake. It's part of the first step,
        origin and importance, and it interacts with layers by flipping their order.
      </p>

      <h2 id="scope">What is @scope proximity?</h2>
      <p>
        <code>
          @scope (.card) {'{'} p {'{'} … {'}'} {'}'}
        </code>{' '}
        limits a rule to elements inside <code>.card</code>. When two scoped rules tie on everything before,
        the one whose scope root is fewer DOM steps away from the element wins. Unscoped rules count as
        infinitely far away.
      </p>
      <p>
        Proximity comes <strong>after</strong> specificity, so a more specific selector beats a closer scope:
      </p>
      <CascadeExample
        caption="Specificity is checked before proximity"
        code={`@scope (.card) {
  p { color: teal; }   /* 1 step from .card */
}
.x p { color: purple; }`}
        rows={[
          row('p', 'teal', { scope: { root: '.card', proximity: 1 }, order: 1 }),
          row('.x p', 'purple', { order: 2 }),
        ]}
      />

      <h2 id="inline">Where do inline styles fit?</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>style</code> attribute isn't a fourth column of specificity. It's a separate step,
        element-attached styles, which runs before layers and specificity. A normal inline style beats every
        normal rule in a stylesheet, even <code>#id#id#id</code>. An inline <code>!important</code> beats any
        author
        <code>!important</code> from a stylesheet.
      </p>
      <CascadeExample
        caption="A normal inline style beats any selector"
        code={`#card#card { color: navy; }
<div id="card" style="color: crimson">`}
        rows={[row('#card#card', 'navy', { order: 1 }), row('', 'crimson', { inline: true, order: 2 })]}
      />
      <p>
        Only an author <code>!important</code> beats a normal inline style, and that's decided at step one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="why-not-applying">Why is my CSS not applying?</h2>
      <p>When a rule seems to be ignored, check these, roughly in the order the cascade does:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The rule is invalid.</strong> A typo, an unknown pseudo-class or a pseudo-element that isn't
          last makes the browser drop the whole rule. The <a href="#panel-anatomy">anatomy</a> view flags
          these.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>
            Something is <code>!important</code>.
          </strong>{' '}
          Normal declarations can never beat it, however specific.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>
            An inline <code>style</code> attribute
          </strong>
          , often set by a script, beats your stylesheet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your rule is in a layer</strong> and the other one isn't, or is in a later layer.
          Specificity never gets a vote.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>An ID is hiding in the other selector.</strong> One ID beats any number of classes. Wrap it
          in
          <code>:where()</code> to neutralise it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>
            A list inside <code>:is()</code> or nesting
          </strong>{' '}
          gave the other rule the specificity of its strongest argument.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The other rule comes later</strong>, in the same file or a stylesheet loaded after yours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It isn't a cascade problem.</strong> The property may not apply to that element, or it's
          inherited from a parent instead of set directly.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The fixes, gentlest first: lower the winner with <code>:where()</code>, move rules into ordered
        layers, raise the loser by repeating a class (<code>.btn.btn</code>) rather than adding an ID, and
        only then reach for
        <code>!important</code>. The duel suggests all four for your selectors.
      </p>
      <p>
        More from the same author on <a href="https://suprabhat-dev.com">suprabhat-dev.com</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
